 that in 2008 had 1.5 billion  records in it so you know these huge  data warehouses are are favored by the  government even if they can't use the  information today they think that some  technological algorithm is going to be  developed that will allow them to see  patterns in the flow of data that allow  them to predict future criminal  activities but you know what I've always  told them is if somebody had you know  some kind of process in which they can  predict the future they wouldn't be  selling it to law enforcement they'd be  selling it to Wall Street and they'd be  selling it to Las Vegas  moved away from surveillance based on  individualized suspicion you know the  Fourth Amendment standard of probable  cause and a warrant has basically  evaporated and the government now can  collect information about people it  doesn't even suspect of wrongdoing a lot  of police departments from all levels of  government federal state and local have  combined their efforts their  intelligence collection efforts and  actually set up facilities called fusion  centers and the problem with these  centers is it's very unclear who's in  charge when you have the FBI sitting  next to the department of homeland  security agent sitting next to a state  police officer sitting next to the local  police officer because there are non law  enforcement agencies working within  these centers military private companies  you know their authority to conduct  domestic law enforcement intelligence  gathering is quite questionable once you  talk about military and private  companies and other non law enforcement  agencies being involved in the center  and receiving this information there  it's very inappropriate and can  potentially have private harms come to  people where say they lose their job  because some fusion center report has  suggested that they're involved with a  group it's dangerous when it's really  just a group that has a policy  difference with the government we have a  number of significant sort of  overarching concerns about the the way  law enforcement intelligence  surveillance authorities have expanded  since 911 the first is the excessive  secrecy that surrounds these activities  the way our government is intended to  function is is by having three branches  of government that check one another too  often what we found is leaks to the news  media or actually provide the  information to Congress to let it know  what's happening rather than the  effective sharing of information between  the branches and also when when people  are harmed by these excessive  surveillance programs and try to sue in  federal court the government has claimed  a state secrets privilege to shut down  the entire lawsuit even where there is  evidence that's not  classified that the person could use to  show they were spied on the government  has not allowed these cases to move  forward so you take two branches of  government out of action and it makes it  much harder to check abuse we need to  have accountability over these agencies  because they all have a long history of  abuse and what we've seen so much since  911 is those guidelines have been  stripped away and they're the agencies  are being told you know in this  emergency environment that you know that  that one piece of data out there might  be the key so go get it so so the  overzealousness that gets created leads  to over collection and encourages  excesses rather than reasonable limits  where the government has a reasonable  factual basis to believe that you are  doing something wrong and our threat to  the community of course we all want to  make sure they have the tools to go and  investigate those people but where the  government has no basis for believing  you're a threat it really should leave  you alone you know the government has no  right to pick through your private  information just because that's  technologically possible and the laws  are now so lacks that they can they can  interview yo